/*
 * consistency.c — cross-language consistency runner for the C binding.
 *
 * Executes the hermetic cases from `tests/consistency/cases.json`
 * (generated by `tests/consistency/generate_cases.py`) and asserts the
 * same golden expectations as the Python / Node.js / Go / Java / .NET /
 * Lua runners. The steps mirror
 * `java/src/test/java/com/kcl/ConsistencyTest.java`:
 *
 *   1. read the manifest and check `version == 1`
 *   2. ask the loaded core for its RPC surface (`BuiltinService.ListMethod`);
 *      a `new_core` case whose rpc is missing is skipped, not failed
 *   3. dispatch on `rpc`, projecting every field the case's `expect`
 *      object pins out of the decoded result
 *   4. compare the pinned field against its projection through one
 *      canonical JSON rendering, on both sides
 *
 * Projections are built as kcl_json_value_t trees (see the jb_* builder)
 * and compared through canonical_render(): object keys are sorted at
 * every depth, `filename` members are dropped (they hold absolute paths,
 * different on every machine), integral numbers render without a
 * fractional part, and a top-level string stays raw so a mismatch diffs
 * line by line. That is the rendering lua's `canonical_json` and go's
 * `normalizeJSON` produce, and the manifest's expectations are pinned to
 * the protobuf-JSON default dialect the core's answers render in: a
 * field the core left unset is absent, not `""`/`0`/`[]`/`null`.
 *
 * Args paths starting with `scratch:` copy
 * `tests/consistency/testdata/<template>` to a fresh directory under
 * $TMPDIR first — the copy is what makes the RPCs that write files safe
 * to run (see make_scratch).
 *
 * Run from the `c` directory — the CI loop in
 * `.github/workflows/c-test.yaml` executes every file under
 * `./examples/` with `c` as the working directory:
 *
 *     make examples && ./examples/consistency
 *
 * Nothing is hardcoded: inputs (code, paths) and expectations both come
 * out of the manifest, so a stale golden value fails the runner rather
 * than passing silently. DISPATCHED_CASES is the hand-maintained
 * coverage guard the sibling ports carry: a manifest case this file does
 * not dispatch fails the run, and so does an entry here the manifest no
 * longer has.
 */

